#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Report every issue a squash-merge will close, refusing a negated one.

A closing keyword (`close`, `fix`, `resolve` and their forms) in a
commit message **body** closes an issue on squash-merge, and
`closingIssuesReferences` cannot see it: that field reads the pull
request body only. So the answer a tool can query and the answer that
acts disagree, and the one that acts is invisible. This reads both.

**GitHub ignores negation.** "fixes #12 and does nothing here" and
"This does not close #12." both close #12. So negation never excuses a
keyword: a parser that honoured the "not" would pass exactly the cases
that fire. It is read in one place only, and only ever adds a refusal:
a body keyword GitHub will act on, in a sentence that denies it, is the
tell that the author did not mean the close GitHub is about to perform.

**Both squash shapes carry the commit body.** At one commit the squash
message is that commit's message verbatim; at two or more the subject
becomes the pull request title and the bodies are concatenated beneath
it, so commit count is no mitigation. **The remedy has to be
pre-merge**: once a keyword is in a merged commit message nobody can
take it out. That is why this is a Merger gate:

    forge-closing-keywords --pr N --include-subject

**`--pr N` names the whole subject**: the pull request's own commits,
its declaration (`closingIssuesReferences`), its title and its body. Its
commits come from GitHub, not from the checkout, because they are what
the squash is built from; a checkout on another branch would answer
about two pull requests at once. A range may be given alongside, to
judge a commit not pushed yet; it is checked against the pull request's
commits, and refused if it is another branch or reaches past the base
(`A...B` is the symmetric difference, and names commits somebody else's
merge already closed). With `--pr` this prints the whole will-close set
and refuses:

* a commit-message keyword closing an issue the pull request does not
  declare (commit bodies; subjects too with `--include-subject`);
* a title keyword, at two or more commits, the body does not declare;
* a body keyword GitHub will act on whose sentence is a denial.

**`--below M` leaves out a merged stack layer's commits.** When the
layer below a stacked pull request has merged, the Merger merges that
layer's final head into it (designs/0025), so the pull request's
commits since the default branch include the lower layer's: the
default branch holds those as one squash commit, not as themselves.
Their keywords closed their issues when that layer merged, and that
layer's own run of this check judged them, so they are no finding here.
`--below M` (one or more, each a merged pull request) drops every
commit that is one of M's. The Merger's gate fills `{below}` with the
merged layer's number, and leaves the argument out when there is none:

    forge-closing-keywords --pr {pr} --include-subject --below {below}

A pull request named that has not merged is exit 2: leaving out an
open one's commits would pass keywords nobody has judged.

A subject keyword is reported, not refused, without
`--include-subject`: a subject is composed, so a keyword there is
usually meant. The gate passes the flag because a subject can also
arrive from another branch, and then only a declaration in the body
says the close is meant.

A keyword naming a pull request rather than an issue closes nothing,
so it is never flagged, and neither is one naming an issue that is
already closed: a commit another pull request brought in and merged
by squash keeps its keyword in this branch's history, but the issue
closed when that one merged, and closing it again does nothing. One
that cannot be looked up is exit 2, since
treating it as either would be wrong in one direction or the other.
When the body declares an issue that `closingIssuesReferences` does not
list yet, the refusal says to wait and run again: that field lags a
body edit by minutes.

**Without `--pr` this does not pretend to have read one**: findings
are reported as half a comparison, and the summary says what was not
read. `--include-subject` without `--pr`, on a branch that has an open
pull request, is refused and names it.

**`--check-body FILE --surface body|commit`** checks text that is not
on GitHub yet: a pull request body, a comment, or a commit message
about to be used with `-F`. A code span disarms a keyword in a body (as
GitHub reads one); in a commit message that is not established, so
there it is reported as acting, and says so. `--declares N...` names
the closes the text means, and only the others are findings.

    forge-closing-keywords                        origin/<default>..HEAD
    forge-closing-keywords --check-body f.md --surface body --declares 12

**Configuration.** The repository whose issues a keyword closes is the
configuration's `repo`; the default range starts at `default_branch`.
`closing_keywords.enforced_by` names what refuses a pull request this
check fails (default "the Merger") and `closing_keywords.rerun` says
how to run it again once GitHub catches up (default: the Merger does,
on its next tick); a project running it in CI names its workflow there.
Reads only, as `$FORGE_AGENT`'s App if set, else `$GH_TOKEN`, which the
Merger sets for its gates, else anonymously.

Exit 0 when nothing closes that is not declared and nothing closes from
a denial, 1 when something does, and 2 when the question could not be
asked: no configuration, an unreadable range or file, a pull request
whose declaration or commits could not be fetched, a range that is not
that pull request's branch, an empty range, or `--include-subject` on a
branch whose open pull request was not named. A range with no commits
closes nothing, which reads exactly like a clean branch, so it is 2,
not 0.
"""

import argparse
import os
import re
import sys
import urllib.parse

sys.path.insert(0, os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(__file__))))
from agent_forge import config, github as forge_github, proc  # noqa: E402

PROG = "forge-closing-keywords"

# Set from the project's configuration by `configure`.
# The repository whose issues a keyword can close from here.
REPO_NAME = "example/widget"
# The commits to judge when neither a range nor `--pr` names any.
DEFAULT_RANGE = "origin/main..HEAD"
# What refuses a pull request this check fails, and how to run it again.
DEFAULT_ENFORCED_BY = "the Merger"
DEFAULT_RERUN = "run the check again (the Merger does, on its next tick)"
ENFORCED_BY = DEFAULT_ENFORCED_BY
RERUN = DEFAULT_RERUN


def closing_re(repo):
    """GitHub's closing keywords, case-insensitive, with an optional
    colon, followed by whitespace and a reference to an issue in `repo`:
    `#N`, a markdown link around `#N`, `owner/name#N`, or the issue's
    URL. Measured on GitHub: `close (#N)`, `close *#N*`, `close "#N"` and
    the like close nothing, so only `[` may displace the whitespace;
    admitting `(` would flag a squash subject's `(#PR)` suffix and every
    "fixes (#1, #2)" citation list. A `/pull/N` URL and another
    repository's `owner/repo#N` close nothing here either. `close [#N]`
    without a link target is flagged though it does not close: a
    spurious flag costs a rewording, a missed one an issue shut by
    accident."""
    return re.compile(
        r"\b(?:close[sd]?|fix(?:e[sd])?|resolve[sd]?):?\s+"
        r"(?:\[?#(\d+)|%s#(\d+)|https?://github\.com/%s/issues/(\d+))\b"
        % (re.escape(repo), re.escape(repo)),
        re.IGNORECASE)


CLOSING_RE = closing_re(REPO_NAME)


def configure(project):
    """Set the module's settings from `project`, a loaded configuration:
    `repo`, `default_branch`, and the `closing_keywords` section's
    `enforced_by` and `rerun`."""
    global REPO_NAME, DEFAULT_RANGE, CLOSING_RE, ENFORCED_BY, RERUN
    section = project.section("closing_keywords")
    REPO_NAME = project.repo
    DEFAULT_RANGE = "origin/%s..HEAD" % project.default_branch
    CLOSING_RE = closing_re(REPO_NAME)
    ENFORCED_BY = section.get("enforced_by", DEFAULT_ENFORCED_BY)
    RERUN = section.get("rerun", DEFAULT_RERUN)


def refs(text):
    """The issue numbers `text`'s closing keywords name, in order. Code
    spans are not skipped here; `blank_code` does that for a body."""
    return [int(next(g for g in m if g)) for m in CLOSING_RE.findall(text)]


CODE_FENCE_RE = re.compile(
    r"^(?P<fence>`{3,}|~{3,})[^\n]*$.*?(?:^(?P=fence)`*~*[^\n]*$|\Z)",
    re.MULTILINE | re.DOTALL)

# A run of N backticks closed by a run of exactly N. It may cross a
# newline but not a blank line, as GitHub renders one: otherwise a stray
# backtick pairs with any later one and blanks the keyword between them,
# and the check passes a body GitHub acts on.
CODE_SPAN_RE = re.compile(
    r"(?<!`)(`+)(?!`)(?:[^\n]|\n(?![ \t]*\n))+?(?<!`)\1(?!`)")


def blank_code(text):
    """`text` with code fences and spans replaced by spaces, newlines kept,
    so every byte outside a code region keeps its line and column."""
    def blank(match):
        return re.sub(r"[^\n]", " ", match.group(0))

    return CODE_SPAN_RE.sub(blank, CODE_FENCE_RE.sub(blank, text))


# Where a `--check-body` text is going. A code span disarms a keyword in
# a pull request body (measured); in a commit message that is not
# established, so the commit surface fails closed: stripping wrongly
# there closes an issue on merge, permanently, where not stripping costs
# a reworded quotation.
SURFACES = ("body", "commit")


def unsafe_pairs(text, surface):
    """Every keyword-then-reference pair in `text` that would act on
    `surface`, as (line, column, issue number, matched text), 1-based."""
    searchable = blank_code(text) if surface == "body" else text
    found = []
    for match in CLOSING_RE.finditer(searchable):
        number = int(next(g for g in match.groups() if g))
        start = match.start()
        line = searchable.count("\n", 0, start) + 1
        column = start - (searchable.rfind("\n", 0, start) + 1) + 1
        found.append((line, column, number, text[start:match.end()]))
    return found


def quoted_pairs(text):
    """The (line, column) of every pair inside a code region. Those are
    still treated as acting on the commit surface; this only lets the
    report say so, so a measured finding and an unmeasured one don't
    print in the same voice."""
    blanked = blank_code(text)
    out = set()
    for match in CLOSING_RE.finditer(text):
        start = match.start()
        if blanked[start] == text[start]:
            continue
        line = text.count("\n", 0, start) + 1
        out.add((line, start - (text.rfind("\n", 0, start) + 1) + 1))
    return out


QUOTED_NOTE = ("    inside a code region: whether GitHub honors one in a "
               "commit message is not established, so this is reported "
               "as acting rather than checked")


def _print_quoted_note(quoted, line, column):
    if (line, column) in quoted:
        print(QUOTED_NOTE)


# A commit's subject is its first line. A keyword there is composed, so
# deliberate; one in the body arrives by accident, inside prose.
SUBJECT_LINES = 1

# What makes a body sentence a denial. Broad on purpose: a false refusal
# costs a rewording, a false pass shuts an issue that should stay open.
NEGATION_RE = re.compile(
    r"\b(?:not|never|no|neither|nor|nothing|without|cannot)\b|n't\b",
    re.IGNORECASE)

# The same question of a commit message, wider, since it only chooses
# which explanation to print for a finding already made.
COMMIT_DENIAL_RE = re.compile(
    NEGATION_RE.pattern + r"|\brather than\b|\binstead of\b",
    re.IGNORECASE)

# The merge commit GitHub synthesizes for a pull_request checkout. No
# squash contains it.
SYNTHETIC_MERGE_RE = re.compile(r"^Merge [0-9a-f]{40} into [0-9a-f]{40}")


class GitHub:
    """Reads from GitHub as one token (None: anonymously). Tests replace
    `call`, which has `agent_forge.github.api_call`'s signature."""

    def __init__(self, token=None, call=forge_github.api_call):
        self.token = token
        self.call = call

    def get(self, path):
        return forge_github.rest(self.token, "GET", path, call=self.call)

    def graphql(self, query, variables):
        return forge_github.graphql(self.token, query, variables,
                                    call=self.call)


def closers(message, body_only=False):
    """Issue numbers `message` would close. `body_only` drops the subject
    line, where a keyword is deliberate."""
    text = message
    if body_only:
        text = "\n".join(message.split("\n")[SUBJECT_LINES:])
    return set(refs(text))


def commit_messages(rng, cwd=None, run=proc.sh):
    """Every (abbreviated sha, message) in `rng`, or None if unreadable."""
    got = run(["git", "-C", cwd or ".", "log", "--format=%h %B%x00", rng])
    if got.returncode != 0:
        return None
    out = []
    for part in got.stdout.split("\0"):
        part = part.strip()
        if not part:
            continue
        sha, _, message = part.partition(" ")
        out.append((sha, message.strip()))
    return out


# How much of a sha to show, so git's and the API's shas print alike.
SHA_WIDTH = 8


# Commits asked for per page of a pull request's list.
PER_PAGE = 100


def pr_commits(pr, github, repo=None):
    """Every (full sha, message) the pull request carries, or None. This is
    the list the squash message is built from. A page that is not a list
    of commits is None, not a shorter list: a list cut short would judge
    part of the pull request as if it were all of it."""
    out, page = [], 1
    while True:
        status, items = github.get(
            "/repos/%s/pulls/%d/commits?per_page=%d&page=%d"
            % (repo or REPO_NAME, pr, PER_PAGE, page))
        if status != 200 or not isinstance(items, list):
            return None
        for item in items:
            try:
                sha, message = item["sha"], item["commit"]["message"]
            except (KeyError, TypeError):
                return None
            if not isinstance(sha, str) or not isinstance(message, str):
                return None
            out.append((sha, message.strip()))
        if len(items) < PER_PAGE:
            return out
        page += 1


def range_shas(rng, cwd=None, run=proc.sh):
    """Every full sha in `rng`, as a set, or None if unreadable."""
    got = run(["git", "-C", cwd or ".", "rev-list", rng])
    if got.returncode != 0:
        return None
    return set(got.stdout.split())


def pr_base_ref(pr, github, repo=None):
    """The name of the branch the pull request merges into, or None. The
    name, not `base.sha`: that is the tip the pull request last synced
    against, and the question is about the tip the merge will use."""
    status, body = github.get("/repos/%s/pulls/%d" % (repo or REPO_NAME, pr))
    if status != 200 or not isinstance(body, dict):
        return None
    ref = (body.get("base") or {}).get("ref")
    return ref.strip() or None if isinstance(ref, str) else None


def not_reachable_from(rng, base, cwd=None, run=proc.sh):
    """Shas in `rng` not already on `base`, or None. A commit on the base
    closes nothing when this pull request merges."""
    got = run(["git", "-C", cwd or ".", "rev-list", rng, "--not", base])
    if got.returncode != 0:
        return None
    return set(got.stdout.split())


def merged_commits(pr, github, repo=None):
    """The full shas of merged pull request `pr`'s commits, as a set, or
    (None, why) when it can't be read or hasn't merged."""
    status, body = github.get("/repos/%s/pulls/%d" % (repo or REPO_NAME, pr))
    if status != 200 or not isinstance(body, dict):
        return None, "could not read pull request %d" % pr
    if not body.get("merged_at"):
        return None, ("pull request %d has not merged, so its commits are "
                      "not judged yet and can't be left out" % pr)
    records = pr_commits(pr, github, repo)
    if not records:
        return None, "could not read pull request %d's commits" % pr
    return set(sha for sha, _m in records), None


def without_below(messages, below, github):
    """(messages less those that are commits of the merged pull requests
    `below`, how many were left out), or (None, why)."""
    shas = set()
    for number in below:
        got, why = merged_commits(number, github)
        if got is None:
            return None, why
        shas |= got
    kept = [(sha, m) for sha, m in messages
            if not any(full.startswith(sha) for full in shas)]
    return kept, len(messages) - len(kept)


def subject_closers(messages):
    """{issue number: [sha, ...]} from commit subjects only, for the
    report. GitHub's synthetic merge commit is skipped."""
    out = {}
    for sha, message in messages:
        if SYNTHETIC_MERGE_RE.match(message):
            continue
        for number in refs("\n".join(message.split("\n")[:SUBJECT_LINES])):
            out.setdefault(number, []).append(sha)
    return out


QUERY = ("query($o:String!,$r:String!,$n:Int!){repository(owner:$o,name:$r)"
         "{pullRequest(number:$n){title body commits{totalCount}"
         " closingIssuesReferences(first:50){nodes{number}}}}}")


def pr_facts(pr, github, repo=None):
    """What the pull request says about itself, or None if unreadable:
    {"declared": closingIssuesReferences' numbers, "title", "body",
    "commits": the count, or None}."""
    owner, _, name = (repo or REPO_NAME).partition("/")
    status, doc = github.graphql(QUERY, {"o": owner, "r": name, "n": pr})
    if status != 200:
        return None
    try:
        node = doc["data"]["repository"]["pullRequest"]
        nodes = node["closingIssuesReferences"]["nodes"]
        declared_ = {n["number"] for n in nodes if "number" in n}
    except (KeyError, TypeError):
        return None
    commits = (node.get("commits") or {}).get("totalCount")
    return {"declared": declared_,
            "title": node.get("title") or "",
            "body": node.get("body") or "",
            "commits": commits if isinstance(commits, int) else None}


def lag_note(number, pr):
    """What to say when the body declares `number` and GitHub's field
    does not list it yet: still a refusal, since the field is what the
    merge acts on, but the remedy is to wait and run again."""
    return ("#%d is declared by a closing keyword in pull request %s's "
            "body, but GitHub's closingIssuesReferences does not list it "
            "yet. That field can lag a body edit by minutes: wait, then "
            "%s. Reword nothing for this." % (number, pr, RERUN))


def field_lags_body(facts):
    """Issues the body's own keywords declare that `closingIssuesReferences`
    does not list yet. The field can lag a body edit by minutes; this
    only chooses the explanation, never the verdict."""
    return set(refs(blank_code(facts["body"]))) - set(facts["declared"])


def declared(pr, github, repo=None):
    """The issues the pull request body declares, or None if unreadable."""
    facts = pr_facts(pr, github, repo=repo)
    return None if facts is None else facts["declared"]


def open_pr_for_head(github, cwd=None, repo=None, run=proc.sh):
    """The open pull request for the checked-out branch, or None for "there
    is none" and "could not tell" alike: refusing because GitHub was
    unreachable would make the offline case the error case. A detached
    HEAD is not asked."""
    got = run(["git", "-C", cwd or ".", "rev-parse", "--abbrev-ref", "HEAD"])
    if got.returncode != 0:
        return None
    branch = got.stdout.strip()
    if not branch or branch == "HEAD":
        return None
    repo = repo or REPO_NAME
    owner, _, _name = repo.partition("/")
    status, body = github.get("/repos/%s/pulls?state=open&head=%s" % (
        repo, urllib.parse.quote("%s:%s" % (owner, branch), safe=":/")))
    if status != 200 or not isinstance(body, list) or not body:
        return None
    number = body[0].get("number") if isinstance(body[0], dict) else None
    return number if isinstance(number, int) and \
        not isinstance(number, bool) else None


def sentences(text):
    """`text` as sentences, with emphasis marks and line wraps removed."""
    flat = re.sub(r"[*_`]", "", text)
    out = []
    for para in re.split(r"\n\s*\n", flat):
        para = " ".join(para.split())
        out.extend(s for s in re.split(r"(?<=[.!?])\s+", para) if s)
    return out


def negated_closers(body, acts_on):
    """[(number, sentence)] for each body keyword GitHub will act on
    (`acts_on`) whose sentence is a denial. The body is read with code
    blanked, so a keyword quoted to explain it is not refused."""
    found = []
    for sentence in sentences(blank_code(body)):
        if not NEGATION_RE.search(sentence):
            continue
        for n in refs(sentence):
            if n in acts_on and (n, sentence) not in found:
                found.append((n, sentence))
    return found


def denying_commits(messages):
    """{issue number: [(sha, sentence), ...]} for commit-message keywords
    whose own sentence denies the close. It only chooses the explanation
    for a finding already made, so it reads the wider COMMIT_DENIAL_RE."""
    found = {}
    for sha, message in messages:
        if SYNTHETIC_MERGE_RE.match(message):
            continue
        for sentence in sentences(message):
            if not COMMIT_DENIAL_RE.search(sentence):
                continue
            for number in refs(sentence):
                found.setdefault(number, []).append((sha, sentence))
    return found


def will_close(facts, messages):
    """Every issue the squash-merge will close, by surface: {"body",
    "title", "commit messages"}, each a set. At one commit the title is
    not used; at two or more it is the squash subject."""
    own = [m for m in messages if not SYNTHETIC_MERGE_RE.match(m)]
    count = facts.get("commits")
    if count is None:
        count = len(own)
    title = (set() if count == 1 else
             set(refs(facts.get("title", ""))))
    from_messages = set()
    for m in own:
        from_messages |= closers(m)
    return {"body": set(facts["declared"]), "title": title,
            "commit messages": from_messages}


def is_issue(number, github, repo=None):
    """True if `number` is an open issue, False if a pull request (which
    a keyword cannot close) or an issue already closed (which the merge
    closes no further), None if the lookup failed: "could not tell" is
    not "is an issue"."""
    status, body = github.get("/repos/%s/issues/%d"
                              % (repo or REPO_NAME, number))
    if status != 200 or not isinstance(body, dict) or "number" not in body:
        return None
    return "pull_request" not in body and body.get("state") != "closed"


def survey(rng=None, pr=None, cwd=None, run=proc.sh, github=None,
           body_only=True, messages=None):
    """(undeclared, from_commits, declared), ("unknown", numbers, None)
    when some number could not be looked up, or None if unaskable.
    `messages` is the commits to judge, when already read."""
    if messages is None:
        messages = commit_messages(rng or DEFAULT_RANGE, cwd=cwd, run=run)
    if messages is None:
        return None
    raw = set()
    for _sha, message in messages:
        raw |= closers(message, body_only=body_only)

    from_commits, unknown = set(), set()
    for number in raw:
        verdict = is_issue(number, github)
        if verdict is True:
            from_commits.add(number)
        elif verdict is None:
            unknown.add(number)
    if unknown:
        return ("unknown", unknown, None)
    if pr is None:
        return (from_commits, from_commits, set())
    known = declared(pr, github)
    if known is None:
        return None
    return (from_commits - known, from_commits, known)


def _numbers(nums):
    return ", ".join("#%d" % n for n in sorted(nums))


def _sentence_start(text):
    return text[:1].upper() + text[1:]


def check_body(path, surface, opener=open, declares=None):
    """Report what `path`'s text would close on `surface`: 0 clean, 1 a
    keyword would act that `declares` does not name (or a body denies a
    close), 2 unreadable. A subject-line keyword on the commit surface
    is reported, not refused: a subject is composed."""
    try:
        with opener(path) as handle:
            text = handle.read()
    except OSError as err:
        print("%s: cannot read %s: %s" % (PROG, path, err), file=sys.stderr)
        return 2

    found = unsafe_pairs(text, surface)
    meant = set(declares or ())
    quoted = quoted_pairs(text) if surface == "commit" else set()

    subject = [f for f in found if surface == "commit" and f[0] == 1]
    acting = [f for f in found if f not in subject and f[2] not in meant]
    agreed = [f for f in found if f not in subject and f[2] in meant]
    undelivered = sorted(meant - {f[2] for f in found})
    denied = (negated_closers(text, {f[2] for f in found})
              if surface == "body" else [])

    for line, column, number, matched in subject:
        print("%s:%d:%d: %r closes #%d from the subject line, where a "
              "keyword is deliberate. Confirm you mean it: after this is "
              "committed the subject cannot be reworded here."
              % (path, line, column, matched, number))
        _print_quoted_note(quoted, line, column)

    for line, column, number, matched in agreed:
        print("%s:%d:%d: %r closes #%d on merge, as declared"
              % (path, line, column, matched, number))
        _print_quoted_note(quoted, line, column)

    for number in undelivered:
        print("  #%d is declared and no keyword here closes it. If the "
              "keyword is in a commit message this is fine -- this mode "
              "reads one file and cannot see one." % number)

    for number, sentence in denied:
        print("%s: #%d would close on merge from a sentence that denies it "
              "-- GitHub ignores negation: %s" % (path, number, sentence))
    if denied:
        print("  %s refuses this body as written. If the close is meant, "
              "give it a sentence of its own, `Fixes #N.`, away from the "
              "negation. If it is not, take the keyword off the number: "
              "other wording, or a code span."
              % _sentence_start(ENFORCED_BY))

    if not acting:
        if denied:
            return 1
        if not subject and not agreed:
            print("%s: %s carries no closing keyword that would act as a %s"
                  % (PROG, path, surface))
            if surface == "body":
                print("  code spans were not searched, which is what lets "
                      "a note quote the keyword it warns about")
        elif agreed:
            print("%s: %d keyword(s) in %s would act as a %s, and every one "
                  "is declared." % (PROG, len(agreed), path, surface))
        return 0

    for line, column, number, matched in acting:
        print("%s:%d:%d: %r would close #%d on merge"
              % (path, line, column, matched, number))
        _print_quoted_note(quoted, line, column)
    print("%s: %d keyword(s) in %s would act as a %s that %s."
          % (PROG, len(acting), path, surface,
             "were not declared" if declares is not None
             else "nothing was declared against"))
    hedged = sum(1 for f in acting if (f[0], f[1]) in quoted)
    if hedged:
        print("  %d of those %s inside a code region: treated as acting "
              "because the question is open, not because it was measured."
              % (hedged, "is" if hedged == 1 else "are"))
    if surface == "body":
        print("  Either declare the close on purpose -- `--declares %s` "
              "here, and a line in the body when you post it -- or put the "
              "keyword inside a code span, which is how a note quotes the "
              "thing it is disarming, and why this check skipped the spans "
              "already there."
              % " ".join(str(f[2]) for f in acting))
    else:
        print("  A commit subject cannot be reworded here without a "
              "force-push, and a code span is not established to disarm "
              "one. Reword before committing: any wording that does not "
              "put a keyword immediately before a reference, such as "
              "\"shuts\" or \"the issue filed as\".")
    return 1


def err(message):
    print("%s: %s" % (PROG, message), file=sys.stderr)


def pr_messages(args, github, run):
    """(messages, what was examined) for `--pr`, or None after saying why
    the pull request's commits could not be judged."""
    records = pr_commits(args.pr, github)
    # A pull request with no commits is not a thing GitHub makes, so an
    # empty list is a read that went wrong quietly.
    if not records:
        err("could not read pull request %s's commits, so what the merge "
            "will close was not judged" % args.pr)
        return None
    if args.range is None:
        return ([(sha[:SHA_WIDTH], m) for sha, m in records],
                "pull request %d's commits" % args.pr)
    have = range_shas(args.range, run=run)
    if have is None:
        err("cannot read %s" % args.range)
        return None
    absent = [sha for sha, _m in records if sha not in have]
    if absent:
        err("%s does not contain %d of pull request %s's %d commit(s) -- "
            "%s -- so it is not that pull request's branch and nothing was "
            "judged. Drop the range: `--pr %s` alone reads the commits from "
            "the pull request."
            % (args.range, len(absent), args.pr, len(records),
               ", ".join(s[:SHA_WIDTH] for s in absent[:3])
               + (", ..." if len(absent) > 3 else ""), args.pr))
        return None
    name = pr_base_ref(args.pr, github)
    if name is None:
        err("could not read pull request %s's base branch, so whether %s "
            "reaches past it was not judged. `--pr %s` alone needs no base, "
            "because it reads the commits from the pull request."
            % (args.pr, args.range, args.pr))
        return None
    base = "origin/%s" % name
    fresh = not_reachable_from(args.range, base, run=run)
    if fresh is None:
        err("cannot read %s, so whether %s reaches past the base was not "
            "judged. `git fetch origin %s` and run it again."
            % (base, args.range, name))
        return None
    mine = set(sha for sha, _m in records)
    behind = have - mine - fresh
    if behind:
        err("%s holds %d commit(s) that are not pull request %s's and are "
            "already on %s, so the merge will not introduce them -- %s. "
            "Nothing was judged, because reporting them would name issues "
            "somebody else's merge already closed. Two dots, not three: "
            "`A...B` is the symmetric difference when it is a commit range, "
            "and includes A's own commits. Or drop the range: `--pr %s` "
            "alone reads the commits from the pull request."
            % (args.range, len(behind), args.pr, base,
               ", ".join(sorted(s[:SHA_WIDTH] for s in behind)[:3])
               + (", ..." if len(behind) > 3 else ""), args.pr))
        return None
    messages = commit_messages(args.range, run=run)
    if messages is None:
        err("cannot read %s" % args.range)
        return None
    return messages, "%s, against base %s" % (args.range, base)


def main(argv=None, run=proc.sh, call=forge_github.api_call,
         load=config.load, environ=None):
    environ = os.environ if environ is None else environ
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(prog=PROG,
                                     description=__doc__.splitlines()[0])
    parser.add_argument("range", nargs="?",
                        help="commits to judge; defaults to "
                             "origin/<default branch>..HEAD, and with --pr "
                             "to the pull request's own")
    parser.add_argument("--pr", type=int,
                        help="the pull request this is about: its commits, "
                             "its declaration, its title and its body")
    parser.add_argument("--include-subject", action="store_true",
                        help="also count the subject line, where a keyword "
                             "is deliberate")
    parser.add_argument("--check-body", metavar="FILE",
                        help="check a text that does not exist on GitHub "
                             "yet: a pull request body, a comment, or a "
                             "commit message about to be used with -F")
    parser.add_argument("--surface", choices=SURFACES,
                        help="where the --check-body text is going; decides "
                             "whether a code span disarms a keyword")
    parser.add_argument("--declares", type=int, nargs="*", metavar="N",
                        help="the issues this text means to close; "
                             "--check-body then reports only the keywords "
                             "you did not declare, which is the verdict "
                             "every other mode gives")
    parser.add_argument("--below", type=int, nargs="*", metavar="M",
                        default=[],
                        help="merged stack layers below the pull request: "
                             "their commits are left out, as their own "
                             "merge already closed what they close")
    args = parser.parse_args(argv)
    if args.below and args.pr is None:
        parser.error("--below leaves a merged layer's commits out of a pull "
                     "request's, so it needs --pr N")

    if args.check_body:
        if args.surface is None:
            parser.error("--check-body needs --surface {%s}: a code span "
                         "disarms a keyword in a pull request body and is "
                         "not established to in a commit message, so the "
                         "same bytes are safe in one and not the other"
                         % "|".join(SURFACES))
    elif args.surface:
        parser.error("--surface only means anything with --check-body")
    elif args.declares is not None:
        parser.error("--declares only means anything with --check-body: "
                     "elsewhere the declaration comes from the pull "
                     "request itself, with --pr N")

    try:
        project = load(environ=environ)
    except config.ConfigError as e:
        err(str(e))
        return 2
    configure(project)
    if args.check_body:
        return check_body(args.check_body, args.surface,
                          declares=args.declares)
    github = GitHub(forge_github.read_token(project, environ, call), call)

    if args.include_subject and args.pr is None:
        mine = open_pr_for_head(github, run=run)
        if mine is not None:
            err("--include-subject judges a commit subject against what a "
                "pull request declares, and no pull request was named. This "
                "branch has one, #%d -- rerun with `--pr %d`. Without it "
                "every keyword reads as undeclared, because nothing was "
                "asked." % (mine, mine))
            return 2

    examined = args.range or DEFAULT_RANGE
    if args.pr is not None:
        got = pr_messages(args, github, run)
        if got is None:
            return 2
        messages, examined = got
        if args.below:
            kept, dropped = without_below(messages, args.below, github)
            if kept is None:
                err("%s, so what the merge will close was not judged"
                    % dropped)
                return 2
            messages = kept
            examined += ", less %d of merged %s's" % (
                dropped, ", ".join("#%d" % n for n in args.below))
    else:
        messages = commit_messages(examined, run=run)
        if messages is None:
            err("cannot read %s" % examined)
            return 2
    if not messages:
        err("%s contains no commits, so nothing was examined" % examined)
        return 2

    got = survey(args.range, pr=args.pr, run=run, github=github,
                 body_only=not args.include_subject, messages=messages)
    if got is None:
        err("could not read what pull request %s declares, so the "
            "comparison was not made" % args.pr)
        return 2
    undeclared, from_commits, known = got
    if undeclared == "unknown":
        err("could not tell whether %s name issues or pull requests, so "
            "nothing was judged. A keyword naming a pull request closes "
            "nothing and must not be flagged; one naming an issue must be. "
            "Treating either as the other is worse than refusing."
            % _numbers(from_commits))
        return 2

    print("  %d commit(s) examined, from %s" % (len(messages), examined))
    if from_commits:
        print("  commit messages would close: %s" % _numbers(from_commits))
    if args.pr:
        print("  the pull request declares:   %s"
              % (_numbers(known) or "(nothing)"))
    # Which remedy to name. A body keyword is somebody writing about this
    # pull request, so declaring it is right. A subject can have arrived
    # from another branch, so the costly remedy is named beside the cheap
    # one. A denial must not be told to declare: that turns the check
    # green and still shuts an issue its author said to leave open.
    carriers = subject_closers(messages)
    deniers = denying_commits(messages)
    lagging = set()
    if args.pr:
        facts = pr_facts(args.pr, github)
        if facts is None:
            err("could not read pull request %s's title and body, so what "
                "the merge will close was not judged" % args.pr)
            return 2
        lagging = field_lags_body(facts)
    for number in sorted(undeclared):
        where = carriers.get(number)
        denied_in = deniers.get(number)
        if args.pr is None:
            print("#%d will close on merge%s. No pull request was named, "
                  "so this was not compared against any declaration -- "
                  "rerun with `--pr N`, which is what decides whether it "
                  "is a finding."
                  % (number, " from %s's subject" % ", ".join(where)
                     if where else ""), file=sys.stderr)
        elif number in lagging and not denied_in:
            print(lag_note(number, args.pr), file=sys.stderr)
        elif where:
            print("#%d will close on merge from %s's subject, which the "
                  "pull request does not declare. Either declare it -- one "
                  "line in the body -- or stop closing it, which means "
                  "rewording that subject on a new branch."
                  % (number, ", ".join(where)), file=sys.stderr)
        elif denied_in:
            sha, sentence = denied_in[0]
            print("#%d will close on merge from a sentence in %s's commit "
                  "message that denies it -- GitHub ignores negation, so "
                  "\"%s\" closes it. Reword so no closing keyword sits in "
                  "front of the number. Do NOT declare it in the body: that "
                  "would make this check pass and still shut an issue you "
                  "have said to leave open."
                  % (number, sha, sentence), file=sys.stderr)
        else:
            print("#%d will close on merge and the pull request does not "
                  "say so" % number, file=sys.stderr)

    negated = []
    undercounted = set()
    undeclared_title = set()
    if args.pr:
        sources = will_close(facts, [m for _sha, m in messages])
        closes, unverified = set(), set()
        for number in set().union(*sources.values()):
            if number in facts["declared"]:
                closes.add(number)
                continue
            verdict = is_issue(number, github)
            if verdict is True:
                closes.add(number)
            elif verdict is None:
                unverified.add(number)
        print("  will close on merge:         %s"
              % (_numbers(closes) or "(nothing)"))
        for source, nums in sources.items():
            if nums & (closes | unverified):
                print("    from the %s: %s"
                      % (source, _numbers(nums & (closes | unverified))))
        if unverified:
            print("    could not tell whether %s name issues"
                  % _numbers(unverified))
        undeclared_title = (sources["title"] & closes) - set(facts["declared"])
        for number in sorted(undeclared_title):
            if number in lagging:
                print(lag_note(number, args.pr), file=sys.stderr)
                continue
            print("#%d will close on merge from the pull request's title, "
                  "which at %d commits is the squash subject, and the body "
                  "does not declare it. Either retitle -- no push needed, "
                  "and nothing else keys on the title -- or declare it in "
                  "the body."
                  % (number, facts.get("commits") or len(messages)),
                  file=sys.stderr)
        undercounted = (closes - set(facts["declared"]) - undeclared
                        - undeclared_title)
        negated = negated_closers(facts["body"], facts["declared"])
        for number, sentence in negated:
            print("#%d will close on merge from a sentence in the body that "
                  "denies it -- GitHub ignores negation: %s"
                  % (number, sentence), file=sys.stderr)
        if negated:
            print("Reword so no closing keyword sits in front of the number, "
                  "then %s." % RERUN, file=sys.stderr)
    from_denial = {n for n, _s in negated} | (set(deniers) & undeclared)
    surface = ("a commit message, subject included" if args.include_subject
               else "a commit body")
    if args.pr is None:
        print("%s: %d issue(s) closed by %s, %d of them from a denial in a "
              "commit message. No pull request was named, so nothing was "
              "compared against a declaration and the body was not read -- "
              "pass `--pr N` for those."
              % (PROG, len(from_commits), surface, len(from_denial)))
    else:
        print("%s: %d issue(s) closed by %s, %d not declared, %d closed from "
              "a denial"
              % (PROG, len(from_commits), surface,
                 len(undeclared | undeclared_title), len(from_denial)))
    # Not an error: a subject is composed, so a keyword in one is usually
    # meant. Saying it and naming the flag that judges it is what is left.
    for number in sorted(undercounted):
        where = carriers.get(number)
        print("%s: #%d will close on merge from a commit subject%s, which "
              "the pull request does not declare. Deliberate, if the keyword "
              "in that subject is meant -- `--include-subject` judges it "
              "rather than reporting it."
              % (PROG, number, " (%s)" % ", ".join(where) if where else ""))
    return 1 if (undeclared or negated or undeclared_title) else 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
